the dynamic memory allocator
============================

yupiter allocates from a private static arena of YUPITER_HEAP_SIZE
bytes (4096 by default).  The heap never asks the operating system
for memory, which is what lets it exist in a freestanding build, and
it makes the worst case leak bounded: a program that loses every
pointer it owns can lose at most the size of the arena, and the
allocator's accounting still reports it.


The public API
--------------

    void *yupiter_malloc(size_t size);
    void  yupiter_free(void *p);
    void *yupiter_realloc(void *p, size_t size);
    void *yupiter_malloc_safe(size_t count, size_t size);
    void  yupiter_free_safe(void *p);
    void *yupiter_realloc_safe(void *p, size_t count, size_t size);

The authoritative contract is the comment above these declarations in
"<yupiter.h>".  In short:

yupiter_malloc
    Returns memory aligned for any object, or null.  A size of 0
    allocates nothing and returns null, which is not a failure.

yupiter_free
    Forgiving by design: a null pointer, a pointer from outside the
    heap, a pointer into the middle of a block or an already free
    block is ignored without modifying anything.  Use the safe
    variant when such a pointer must be reported instead.

yupiter_realloc
    Resizes a live block to "size" bytes and keeps its contents up to
    the smaller of the old and the new size.  A null pointer allocates,
    and a size of 0 releases and returns null, as C17 permits.  Growth
    into the free space after the block and shrinking into a new free
    tail happen in place; only a block that cannot be resized that way
    is copied into a fresh allocation and released.  Returns null on
    failure, leaving the original block live and unchanged.  Added
    bytes are not initialised.

yupiter_malloc_safe
    Checks that "count * size" does not overflow, and zeroes the
    memory it returns.  A request it cannot satisfy - a wrapping size
    or an exhausted heap - writes a diagnostic to yupiter_stderr and
    terminates the process with status 134.  The only null result is
    the documented "nothing was requested" case.

yupiter_free_safe
    Same validation as yupiter_free, but a pointer that is not the start
    of a live block is reported: diagnostic to yupiter_stderr, then
    termination with status 134.  A null pointer still releases
    nothing and is not an error.

yupiter_realloc_safe
    Same as yupiter_realloc, with the checks of the safe family: the
    "count * size" product is checked for overflow, and an invalid
    pointer, an overflowing size or an exhausted heap is fatal instead
    of returning null.  A null pointer allocates like
    yupiter_malloc_safe, including its zero fill; growing an existing
    block leaves the added bytes uninitialised.  A zero count or size
    releases the block and returns null, which is not an error.

The heap is not thread-safe: yupiter creates no threads and provides
no synchronisation.


Sizing
------

YUPITER_HEAP_SIZE is defined in include/yupiter_mem.hxx with a default of
4096 and may be overridden without editing a header:

    ./configure --heap-size=1024
    cc -DYUPITER_HEAP_SIZE=8192 ...

The value must be a plain decimal number of bytes and at least 64; the
configuration guard rejects anything else when the project is
configured.  Static assertions in mem/heap.cxx reject a value that is
not positive, does not fit in a size_t, or is smaller than one rounded
block header plus one alignment unit, so a build that bypasses ./configure
cannot produce an allocator with no room for a block.  The tests do not
depend on the value: they ask yupiter_heap_capacity what the arena holds
and shape each scenario to fit.


Layout of the arena
-------------------

The arena is one contiguous run of blocks.  Every block starts with a
header, followed by its payload:

    arena (YUPITER_HEAP_SIZE bytes, aligned for any object)

    +-------------------+-------+-------+-------------------+-----
    | payload (size_t)  | state | magic | payload bytes ... |
    +-------------------+-------+-------+-------------------+-----
    ^ block start                                ^ payload:
                                                  what malloc returns

    payload    usable bytes after the header, always a multiple of
               the alignment
    state      free or used
    magic      0x59555049 ("YUPI"): marks bytes as a header this
               allocator wrote

The header is rounded up to alignof(std::max_align_t) - 16 bytes on
aarch64 and on x86_64 - and every payload size is a multiple of that
alignment too, so a payload is suitably aligned for any object.  A size request
is rounded up the same way before it is looked for.  The state field
is a scoped enumeration with a fixed 32-bit underlying type, so the
on-arena layout is the same on every target.


Allocation
----------

1. A size of 0 returns null immediately.  Otherwise the size is
   rounded up to the alignment; a size that would wrap while being
   rounded fails without scanning the arena.
2. On the first use the arena becomes a single free block spanning it
   all.
3. The block list is scanned first-fit for a free block whose payload
   is large enough.
4. The chosen block is split when the leftover can hold another block
   (a header plus one aligned payload); a smaller leftover stays with
   the block rather than becoming an unusable fragment.
5. The block becomes used, the accounting is increased, and the
   payload is returned.

A request larger than the arena can hold fails without scanning.


Resizing
--------

yupiter_realloc first locates and validates the block, then takes one of
three paths:

  1. Shrink: when the requested payload leaves a remainder large
     enough for another block, the block keeps its address, its payload
     shrinks and a free tail is written after it.  A remainder smaller
     than a header plus one alignment unit stays with the block, so a
     later growth does not have to move it.
  2. Grow in place: every free block after the block is absorbed until
     the payload is large enough.  When the result leaves a usable
     remainder, that remainder is split back into a free tail.
  3. Move: when the following block is in use and the free space after
     it is still too small, a new block is allocated, the old contents
     are copied, and the old block is released.

The shrink and grow paths keep the "no two adjacent free blocks"
invariant: a free tail is merged with any free block that follows it.
The move path is the only one that changes the address, and it is also
the only one that can need memory elsewhere in the arena while the old
block is still live.  A failed move returns null (or terminates, for
yupiter_realloc_safe) with the original block untouched.

The bytes a growing resize adds are not initialised.  yupiter_realloc_safe
can zero a fresh allocation, through its null-pointer case, but the
allocator does not keep the requested size of a live block, so it cannot
tell which bytes of an existing block were beyond the caller's last
request; initialise them explicitly when an extension has to be zero.


Release and merging
-------------------

The block list maintains one invariant: no two adjacent blocks are
free.  A split leaves the tail free next to a block that stays in use,
and a release absorbs the free blocks on both sides of the block being
released, so the invariant holds before and after every operation.  It
is what lets a release do all of its work in the single walk that finds
the block, instead of walking the arena a second time to coalesce.

Before anything is touched, the pointer is classified:

    outside the arena or misaligned   ignored / reported
    not the start of a block          ignored / reported
    block already free                double free
    header does not add up            heap block is corrupted

The check that rejects a pointer into the middle of a block is a walk
of the block list: only offsets the list actually steps over are
accepted, and a payload size that does not add up stops the walk
instead of running off the end of the arena.  The same walk reports the
block that precedes the one being released, which is the only free
block that can be on that side.

The neighbours are then validated as well - the free block before, and
the run of free blocks after - before a single byte of the arena is
written, so a corrupt list is reported with the list untouched.  Only
then is the released block marked free and the neighbours absorbed into
it.

The headers of absorbed blocks stay behind as stale bytes inside the
merged payload.  They read as free, so a double free of a block that a
merge absorbed is still recognised instead of looking like a pointer
into a payload, and the list walk never lands on them again.  The
accounting is decreased by the payload of the released block alone,
which is what keeps two releases of blocks that later merged from
giving back the same bytes twice.

Releasing everything returns the arena to a single free block; the
tests prove it by asking afterwards for an allocation that only the
whole arena can serve.


Failure modes of the safe variants
----------------------------------

Each of these writes a line to yupiter_stderr and exits with status 134
(the conventional status of an aborted process, 128 + SIGABRT):

    yupiter: yupiter_malloc_safe: allocation size overflows
    yupiter: yupiter_malloc_safe: out of memory
    yupiter: yupiter_free_safe: pointer is outside the yupiter heap
    yupiter: yupiter_free_safe: pointer is not the start of a heap block
    yupiter: yupiter_free_safe: block is already free (double free)
    yupiter: yupiter_free_safe: heap block is corrupted
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: allocation size overflows
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: out of memory
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: pointer is outside the yupiter heap
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: pointer is not the start of a heap block
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: block is already free (double free)
    yupiter: yupiter_realloc_safe: heap block is corrupted

The diagnostic goes to yupiter_stderr, which is unbuffered, so it
reaches the operating system before the exit system call.

Because a failure ends the process, these paths are tested one
scenario per run: test/heap_abort.c selects the scenario on the
command line and test/run-tests.sh asserts the status.  See
docs/testing.


Accounting (internal)
---------------------

include/yupiter_mem.hxx declares three queries used by the tests:

    size_t yupiter_heap_live_bytes(void);    payload bytes reserved
    size_t yupiter_heap_live_blocks(void);   blocks they live in
    size_t yupiter_heap_capacity(void);      size of the arena

Both counts are zero once every allocation has been released, which
is what the tests assert after each scenario.  The header is not part
of the public API and is not installed.


Who allocates inside the library
--------------------------------

Only the formatting engine: every formatted call borrows one block of
97 bytes (65 for digit storage, 32 for the padding chunk) and gives it
back before returning, falling back to stack buffers when the heap is
exhausted.  Nothing else in the library allocates - in particular the
stream staging buffer stays inside the stream object.  See
docs/formatting.


Design notes and limits
-----------------------

  * No system call is used, so the allocator behaves identically on
    any operating system that builds the library, and nothing here
    needs to grow the heap.
  * Inside mem/heap.cxx the arena - a declared "std::byte" array - is
    only read and written through byte lvalues, and block headers are
    moved with yupiter_memcpy rather than by casting the storage to a
    structure pointer, so strict aliasing is respected for everything
    the library itself does with it.  What a caller does with the
    payload it receives is the caller's own effective type, as with
    any other allocator.
  * A pointer that is freed after its offset has been handed out again
    to a later allocation cannot be told apart from a valid pointer of
    that later allocation.  No allocator can: the information is not
    in the pointer.  Double frees of a block that has not been reused,
    invalid pointers, interior pointers and misaligned pointers are
    all detected.
  * The tests never allocate more than a few kilobytes, so a leak in a
    test stays that small by construction, and the accounting turns it
    into a failing check anyway.

Non-terminating checked operations
---------------------------------

yupiter_free_checked(p) and yupiter_realloc_checked(p, size, &result)
return yupiter_memory_status: OK, INVALID_POINTER, OUT_OF_MEMORY, or
OVERFLOW (with the YUPITER_MEMORY_ prefix).  They never invoke the fatal
handler.  The resize follows the same content and zero-size rules as
yupiter_realloc, clears result on failure, and leaves the original live
block unchanged.  Result storage must be writable and outside the heap.
A null free is successful; an invalid or corrupt pointer is rejected.
These additive interfaces support hosted language bindings while the
existing native safe functions retain their status-134 failure policy.
